course: dictionary
lesson: 13952
title: Freedom fighters and their words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A liberator is a person, army or group that sets people or a place free from enemies, slavery or prison. The word is built from liberate, which means to set free, so a liberator is the one who does the freeing.
explain: Read this: «The soldiers were hailed as liberators when they marched into the town.» The people cheered them because they had freed the town from enemy control. Hailed means cheered or praised. The clue is that the town was saved, so liberators must be the ones who saved it.
explain: Liberator is a naming word: you can say a liberator, or many liberators. In history books, «the Liberator» is a title given to Simón Bolívar, who helped free several countries in South America. When you see a word with a capital letter like this, it may be a special name, so check the sentence for clues.
fact: A liberator is a person, army or group that sets people or a place free.
fact: Liberators can free people from enemies, from slavery or from prison.
fact: «Liberate» means to set free, and a liberator is the one who does it.
fact: «Hailed as a liberator» means that people cheered and praised someone for freeing them.
fact: «The Liberator» is a title used for Simón Bolívar, who helped free several South American countries.
exercise: «The soldiers were hailed as liberators when they marched into the town.» What is a liberator? | A soldier who starts a war | A town full of visitors | A guard who locks the prison gates | *A person, army or group that sets people free
exercise: «The crowd cheered their liberator as he opened the prison gates.» What does liberator mean here? | A cook who makes big meals | A person who locks people in | *A person who sets people free from prison | A teacher of history
exercise: Why is Simón Bolívar called «the Liberator»? | He was a king who never left his palace | He wrote the most famous children's book | *He helped set several South American countries free | He was the first person to build a railway in Europe
test: «The villagers hailed the army as liberators after the long war.» What does hailed mean? | Blamed and scolded | *Cheered and praised | Ignored completely | Chased away
test: Which word means a person or group that sets people free? | *Liberator | Captive | Guard | Prisoner
exam: «After years of fear, the people welcomed their liberators with flags and songs.» Who were the liberators? | The children who sang the songs | *The people or army who set them free from fear and enemy rule | Visitors who came to see the castle | Enemies who took the town
